# Seven notes. Three are shown to the default reader: the OTOC and its bound, the KAM theorem (the paper's classical
# point of comparison), and the symmetry criterion for exact marginality in supersymmetric theories. Three sit in
# the introduction (SYK, the OTOC, KAM); the others follow the first body paragraph that works with the idea. Listed in reading order.
BACKGROUND = [
    {"id": "bg-syk", "after": "section-1-p3", "title": "Disorder averaging and the SYK model", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>In the SYK model, a disorder model, each fermion interacts with all others through couplings that are random and of size $1/N^{(q-1)/2}$. Averaging over the couplings pairs them up, and at large $N$ only a special class of Feynman diagrams survives: the melons, in which every propagator is dressed by the same self-energy. The full propagator then obeys a closed Schwinger–Dyson equation, $\Sigma=J^2G^{q-1}$ together with $G=1/(G_0^{-1}-\Sigma)$. At strong coupling the free term $G_0^{-1}$ can be dropped, the equation becomes scale invariant, and a power-law ansatz solves it, with dimension $1/q$. The four-point function at order $1/N$ is a sum of ladders built from the same melons, summed through the eigenvalues of one kernel. The paper asks how much of this structure survives when the free fermions are replaced by an interacting core CFT.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-otoc", "after": "section-1-p8", "title": "Out-of-time-order correlators and the bound on chaos", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>In classical chaos, nearby trajectories separate as $e^{\lambda t}$. The quantum analogue compares a perturbation $W$ applied at time $t$ with a probe $V$ at time zero, through the squared commutator $\langle[W(t),V]^2\rangle$. Expanded, it contains correlators whose operators are not time ordered, hence the name out-of-time-order correlators. In large-$N$ systems the squared commutator starts at order $1/N$ and grows as $e^{\lambda_Lt}/N$, until it becomes of order one at the scrambling time, about $\lambda_L^{-1}\log N$. Maldacena, Shenker and Stanford proved that in a thermal state $\lambda_L\le2\pi/\beta$. The bound is saturated by black holes in Einstein gravity and by the SYK model at strong coupling, while weakly coupled theories have small $\lambda_L$. The paper follows $\lambda_L$ between these two ends along a line of conformal theories.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-kam", "after": "section-1-p10", "title": "Integrable systems, invariant tori and the KAM theorem", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>A classical integrable system with $n$ degrees of freedom has $n$ conserved quantities, and its motion winds around $n$-dimensional invariant tori in phase space with fixed frequencies; nothing is chaotic. The Kolmogorov–Arnold–Moser theorem asks what a small perturbation does. If the unperturbed frequencies change with the tori (non-degeneracy), most tori, those whose frequency ratios are far enough from rational, survive, only deformed; the measure of destroyed tori goes to zero with the perturbation. Chaos appears first in thin layers around the destroyed resonant tori, and widespread chaos requires a finite perturbation. The theorem needs finitely many degrees of freedom, and its bounds on the perturbation are often very small; the Fermi–Pasta–Ulam–Tsingou chain shows regular behaviour persisting beyond them. The paper's discontinuous onset of quantum chaos is its loose analogue.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-marginal", "after": "section-2.5.1-p1", "title": "Classically and exactly marginal deformations", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>Deforming a conformal field theory by $g\int d^dx\,\mathcal O$, with $\mathcal O$ of dimension exactly $d$, is classically marginal: $g$ is dimensionless. Quantum corrections decide whether it stays so. In conformal perturbation theory the beta function starts as $\beta_g\propto C_{\mathcal{OOO}}\,g^2$, with $C_{\mathcal{OOO}}$ the coefficient of $\mathcal O$ in its own operator product expansion. If it is non-zero, $g$ runs, and one sign makes the deformation marginally irrelevant: it dies away logarithmically at long distances, as many disorder couplings of free fields do. If the beta function vanishes to all orders, the deformation is exactly marginal and $g$ labels a family of conformal theories, a conformal manifold. Exactly marginal deformations are rare without supersymmetry. The paper uses them to keep every value of the disorder $J$ conformal.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-gff", "after": "section-3.1-p1", "title": "Generalized free fields", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>A free field of dimension $\Delta$ is generalized when $\Delta$ is not the free value, $(d-2)/2$ for a scalar in $d$ dimensions: its two-point function is the conformal $1/|x|^{2\Delta}$, and every higher correlator is the sum over Wick pairings of two-point functions. This defines a consistent conformal theory, but a non-local one: its quadratic action has the kernel $1/|x-y|^{2d-2\Delta}$ in place of a derivative, and it has no stress tensor. Generalized free fields appear as the large-$N$ limit of single-trace operators in holographic theories, and in quantum mechanics as fermions with a non-local kinetic term. Because their correlators are all known, a disordered theory built on them can be solved exactly, which is what the cSYK model of Gross and Rosenhaus does with fermions of dimension $1/q$.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-n2", "after": "section-3.2.1-p6", "title": "N=(2,2) minimal models as Landau–Ginzburg theories", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>A $1+1$d theory of one chiral superfield $\Phi$ with superpotential $W=\Phi^q$ is a Landau–Ginzburg model. It is not conformal, but it flows to a superconformal fixed point, the $\mathcal N=(2,2)$ $A_{q-1}$ minimal model, with central charge $3(1-2/q)$. Supersymmetry protects the chiral ring: the superpotential has R-charge 2, so $\Phi$ has R-charge $2/q$, and chiral primaries in $1+1$d have dimension equal to half their R-charge, so $\Phi$ keeps dimension $1/q$ exactly. Its chiral primaries are the powers $\Phi^k$ with $k<q-1$, since $\Phi^{q-1}$ vanishes in the chiral ring. These minimal models are also exactly solvable conformal theories, with known correlators. The paper couples $N$ of them through products of $\Phi$'s from $q$ different copies, a superpotential of dimension one, which is classically marginal.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-symmetry", "after": "section-3.2.1-p8", "title": "Exactly marginal couplings and broken global symmetry", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>In supersymmetric theories with four supercharges, such as $4$d $\mathcal N=1$ or $1+1$d $\mathcal N=(2,2)$, a classically marginal superpotential coupling can fail to be exactly marginal in one way only. If the coupling breaks a continuous global symmetry, the broken current is no longer conserved, and it combines with the marginal operator into a longer multiplet that is marginally irrelevant. Green, Komargodski, Seiberg, Tachikawa and Wecht (and earlier Kol) showed that the conformal manifold is the space of marginal couplings divided by the complexified global symmetry group. So if the undeformed theory has no continuous non-R global symmetry, there is nothing to break, and every classically marginal coupling is exactly marginal. The $N$ decoupled minimal models have no such symmetry, which is why every realization of the paper's random couplings is a conformal theory.</p>"""},
]
